-- lib/repeats-fast.cub — the suffix array of lib/repeats.cub by prefix doubling (Manber and Myers 1990),
-- proved equal to it (PR #10, item 3, second pass, 2026-11-17). lib/repeats.cub's suffix-array (merge
-- sort comparing suffixes token by token) is the specification; here:
--   ranks          ranks t: round 1 ranks each suffix by its first token; round k+1 ranks it by the pair
--                  (its rank, the rank of the suffix 2^k further on, #0 past the end), so after round k
--                  the ranks order suffixes by their first 2^k tokens; rounds stop once 2^k ≥ the text's
--                  length. A rank is one more than the number of keys below it (count-lt), read from a
--                  search tree over the sorted keys (build, cnt-tree): one sort of word pairs and n
--                  lookups per round, no comparison of suffixes.
--   proofs         tree-cnt: the tree counts the keys below a key. rank-prop: ranks compare as their keys.
--                  lex-double: comparing the first 2h tokens is comparing the first h, then the next h.
--                  round-ok: the invariant (DblInv) passes from h to 2h. loop-ok and ranks-ok: the final
--                  ranks compare as the whole suffixes do.
--   the array      suffix-array-fast t: the entries sorted by their final rank.
--                  suffix-array-fast-is: it is suffix-array t (sorted-unique: both are sorted and hold the
--                  same entries), so suffix-array-sorted, suffix-array-permutation and every theorem of
--                  lib/repeats.cub hold of it; suffix-array-fast-sorted and -permutation state two.
import "repeats.cub"

